# Meta-Rules: Writing Cursor Rules and Skills

## Language and Communication

- **Language of rules and skills**: all text in `.mdc` files and `SKILL.md` is always **technical English** — concise, clear, no filler. No translation comments, no second-language copy.
- **Language of code**: identifiers, docstrings, inline comments, and commit messages are always **English**.
- **Chat with the user**: always in `<communication-language>` (see `00-communication-language.mdc`), unless the user writes otherwise.

## `.mdc` File Structure

```markdown
---
description: Brief rule description (1–2 sentences, English)
globs: **/*.ts        # file pattern; omit if alwaysApply: true
alwaysApply: false    # true = applies regardless of context
---

# Rule Title

## Section

- Bullet point in English.
```

## Rule Writing Principles

### Conciseness and Focus

- One rule = **one concern** (one file = one topic).
- **Line limit depends on activation type** (cost rationale below):
  - `alwaysApply: true` → **strict 150 lines** — loaded on **every** AI call; every extra line costs tokens.
  - `globs` / auto-attached → **250 lines** acceptable — loaded only when matching files are in context.
  - Move anything longer to a skill's `reference.md`.
- No explaining the obvious — the agent is an expert.
- Use **❌ / ✅ code examples** instead of vague instructions.

### Activation Type (choose deliberately)

| Type | When to use |
|------|------------|
| `alwaysApply: true` | Global conventions always active (language, git, workflow) |
| `globs: **/*.py` | File-type-specific rules — saves context window |
| `description` without globs | Agent-requested: AI determines relevance |
| `@mention` in chat | Manual activation — only when explicitly invoked |

Prefer **auto-attached** rules via `globs` over `alwaysApply: true` — saves context window.

### Security Boundaries (2026 agentic engineering)

- Every workflow rules file **must** contain a guard against autonomous destructive actions.
- Mandatory in workflow rules:
  - No `git commit/push` without explicit user consent.
  - No deletion of config files without confirmation.
  - No pipeline/DB operations without approval.

### File Naming

```
00-communication-language.mdc   # source of truth for language — always first
00-meta-rules-and-skills.mdc
01-general-programming.mdc
02-git.mdc
10-python.mdc
10-typescript.mdc
20-docker.mdc
```

## SKILL.md Structure

```markdown
---
name: skill-name              # max 64 chars, kebab-case
description: >-
  What this skill does.       # max 1024 chars — critical for AI discovery!
  Use when: <trigger phrase>. # explicitly state when to use this skill.
---

# Skill Title

## Prerequisites

## Steps

## Additional resources
- [reference.md](reference.md)
```

### Rules for Skills

- `description` is critical: the agent finds a skill only if the description contains keywords from the request.
- `SKILL.md` max **500 lines**; move the rest to `reference.md` or `examples.md`.
- One skill = one workflow. Prefer multiple focused skills over one "mega-skill".
- If the skill calls commands, provide **exact commands** with expected output.

## Pre-save Checklist

- [ ] `.mdc` file is in `.cursor/rules/` with correct frontmatter
- [ ] `globs` or `alwaysApply` is set deliberately
- [ ] Length: under **150 lines** for `alwaysApply: true`; under **250 lines** for `globs` rules; under **500 lines** for skills
- [ ] Contains concrete examples (❌ / ✅) or commands
- [ ] English only — no translation comments in rules or skills
- [ ] No vague instructions — only concrete, actionable rules
